00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:03.742
one of the real
leaders in this area.

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:06.200
And then finally, we have a
lot of backup and archiving use

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.710
cases, as well.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.240
So here you see just a
handful of household names

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.840
that use Cloud Storage
in each of these areas.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.430
So let's dig into, now, some
of the most recent changes

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.060
in storage classes.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.000
So storage classes
really dictate

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.100
how we place your
data, what regions,

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.490
and then, of course,
what the hotness

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:31.850
and coldness of the data,
how frequently you access it.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:39.940
And up until today, actually,
we've had a mix of location

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:44.020
with hotness and coldness of
data in the storage class name.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.950
So we've had this general
concept of storage class.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.670
Multi-Regional and Regional,
were both the hottest classes.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:54.550
But those referred
to the locations

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.580
where Multi-Regional
was geo-redundant

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.890
and Regional locked down the
data to a specific location.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.840
And then we had Nearline and
Coldline for the archival use

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:05.710
cases.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.110
But you could actually also
use those in a Regional

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.093
or Multi-Regional location.

00:05:10.093 --> 00:05:12.010
So we felt like this was
actually a little bit

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.000
confusing to customers.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.060
And so we're in the
middle of rolling out

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:19.367
changes that will make
this a little bit simpler.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:20.950
So basically, what
we're looking to do

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.350
is we're going to be
separating out the location

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.140
type from the storage
class and only

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.120
using storage classes to
mean the data temperature,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.120
the frequency of access.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.060
So Standard is the most
frequently accessed.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.650
Then, as the data
gets colder, you

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.930
go to Nearline and
then go to Coldline.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.780
And it's a consistent
set of APIs,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.450
no matter what storage
class you're using.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:45.280
And then similarly,
on location type,

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.510
this really dictates the
amount of geo-redundancy

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.580
and where your data is
stored generally, right?

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.070
So if you're stored
in a region, you'll

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.082
know that all of your data
is in that specific region.

00:05:57.082 --> 00:05:58.540
If you're in a
Multi-Region, you'll

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.110
be aligned to a continent.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.400
So this will generally be, for
example, the United States,

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.520
the European Union, or
the Asia-Pacific region.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.610
And then your data will be at
least in two places somewhere

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.110
in that continent.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:12.820
And then dual
regions are the best

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.000
of both worlds, where you know
exactly where your data is.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.260
It's in two specific regions.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:22.280
And that allows you to co-locate
your compute with your storage.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.390
And if you go up and down
within that stack from Standard

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:29.720
to Nearline to Coldline, the
geo-redundancy is preserved.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.930
So in the past,
Nearline and Coldline

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.330
had regional
characteristics, even

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:37.030
if it was stored in a
multi-regional location.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.920
So that's something that we've
improved as of last October,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.180
that now the geo-redundancy
stays consistent

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.730
as you go up and down
within your buckets.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.500
So here are examples of
regions, multi-regions,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.990
and dual regions, going
back to that globe map.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:01.320
So the big green
circles there are

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.000
illustrating the US and the
Asia-Pacific multi-regions.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.210
There are two smaller ovals that
are dual regions, one in Europe

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.790
and one in North America,
where those are locked down

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.420
to two specific regions.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.110
And then all of the
individual spots

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:21.620
there are individual regions
that we provide service in.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.220
So why pick a particular
location type?

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:29.480
So regions offer the
lowest possible price point

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.540
and are specifically locked
around one data center.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.040
So if you have jurisdictional
reasons, for example,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.440
you want to make sure the data
stays in a particular country,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.520
for example, especially
in Asia or in Europe,

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.995
that's one popular reason
to choose regional.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.860
The other approach is
basically for co-location,

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:55.670
as you'll hear as we get into
some of the other specific use

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:56.960
cases.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.460
Dual regions keeps
it locked down,

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:01.610
like I said, but with actually
two regions in the mix.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:05.060
That gives you the higher
availability characteristics

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.300
without sacrificing the
knowledge that your data is

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.440
in specific locations.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.230
But if you don't care
where your data is,

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:14.790
you just want it to be
very high availability

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.780
and you want it to be stored
in more than one region,

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.710
multi-region basically gives you
a price cut from dual regions.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.810
And it gives Google
the freedom to store

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:27.020
your data in whatever data
centers within that region,

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.260
as makes sense.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.880
So particularly for
content delivery,

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.140
we see a lot of use
of multi-regions.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.169
So this is what happens
with data placement.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.450
If you pick a region,
all of your objects

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:39.510
end up in that region.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.770
So the green boxes
here under Region B

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.530
assume that you've selected
Region B as your region

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:48.500
for that particular bucket.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.190
If you picked a multi-region
in the red color,

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.140
here you can see that
different objects--

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.000
they're still in at
least two regions,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.632
but it's basically
randomized per object.

00:08:59.632 --> 00:09:02.090
So you don't really know, for
any given object, what region

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.048
they're going to be in.

00:09:03.048 --> 00:09:04.940
But you know that
they're geo-redundant.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.530
Whereas here again,
for the dual region,

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.950
this dual region happens
to be Region B and C,

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.500
and so your objects are
always in those two locations.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.210
So a big announcement
that we made this morning

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:23.260
is that we're actually adding
a new archive storage class.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.840
So this is directly
aimed at tape replacement

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.860
needs and other very long
term storage requirements.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.890
So if you're planning on
keeping your data around for 3,

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.085
5, 7, 10 years, don't
expect to access it,

00:09:37.085 --> 00:09:38.710
would have considered
to put it on tape

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:41.140
before, this is
an ideal solution

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.610
for those sorts of needs.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:48.790
So this really just adds another
tier to the storage classes.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.670
And basically, you'll be
able to go from Standard

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:55.390
to Nearline to Coldline, and
now, to Archive and have,

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.240
really, a full suite of
storage class selections

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.380
for all of your needs.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.230
And as you can
see, we're talking

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:06.580
about starting at a
price point of $0.12.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:09.290
So this is a very
appealing price point.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.330
But at the same time, this
is not a storage class

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.560
that's glacially slow.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.470
It has the same performance
characteristics as all

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:19.070
the other storage classes.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:21.560
So you don't need to
actually restore data.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.287
So if you have 10
petabytes of storage

00:10:24.287 --> 00:10:25.870
and that was stored
on tape, you might

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.420
be talking about weeks of
time to restore the data.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:32.800
In this case, the data is right
there, immediately available

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:33.590
for your access.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.700
You don't have to first
restore it to another bucket

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.610
under another storage class
to be able to access it.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.880
It's available directly
out of the Archive class.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.640
And we expect that,
even though this is data

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.370
that you don't want
to ever access,

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.170
if you do need to
access it, like there's

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.840
a regulatory reason that
you need to suddenly get

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.210
your data back and run
some analytics, here

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:57.730
you won't have to go
through a week long,

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.460
multi-week long period to
get access to your data.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.820
And it's also stored with
very high durability.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.640
One of the problems with tape
historically has been you

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.320
really have to write
it to tape, you

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.140
have to validate that it's
been correctly written.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.840
And it's often the case that
when people go back to tapes,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.120
that they find that their
data isn't there the way

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.560
they thought it is.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:20.800
And tape libraries can
be very finicky devices.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:24.310
So with Cloud Storage, and if
you select the Archive class,

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:25.960
it's stored with
the same 11 nines

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:29.650
durability that you've come to
expect from all of our storage

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.070
classes.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.280
So we're very excited
about this announcement.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:35.960
Archive will be available
later this year.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.370
So stay tuned for availability.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:40.870
And if you do choose
Archive, I want

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:43.480
to mention, in multi-regions
or in dual regions,

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.770
same level of geo-redundancy
applies as the other location

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.470
types would imply--
so very consistent

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.930
across-- consistent
APIs, consistent level

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:56.568
of geo-redundancy.

00:11:56.568 --> 00:11:58.860
So what are the best practices
on selecting the storage

00:11:58.860 --> 00:11:59.560
classes?

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.790
So hopefully you've already
selected a location type

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.280
that's appropriate for
the amount of availability

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.500
that your application
cares about.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.220
But basically, it's a
combination of retention period

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.180
and access frequency.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.750
So if you're going to
access data very frequently,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.850
or if you're not going
to keep it very long,

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:19.920
then the Standard class
is going to give you

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.140
the best possible price point.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.030
Whereas if you're going to
store it for, let's say,

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:28.050
10 years and never read
it, then clearly Archive

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.530
will be the best choice.

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:32.580
And in the middle fit
Nearline and Coldline.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.910
So generally speaking,
if you're accessing it

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.280
less frequently than
once a year and storing

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:40.710
for more than 12 months,
that's where the new Archive

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.030
class fits in.

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.540
But we have a lot of use
of Nearline and Coldline,

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:48.150
especially for long
tail content archives

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:51.840
where the data is expected to
be read, maybe every month,

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:52.870
on average.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.950
And that's where those
middle tiers make sense.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.020
And then to unify
all of this, it's

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:03.670
great to have a consistent API.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:07.180
But it's also useful to have
a policy driven mechanism

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.780
for down leveling the
tiers of your storage

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:12.460
as the data gets colder.

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:16.210
And so we provide that with
the Object Lifecycle Manager.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.780
You can basically specify
based on the age of objects.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:22.030
Usually, that
correlates very well

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.340
with the frequency of access.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:27.010
So based on your data set,
if you understand that,

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:28.210
in this example--

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:31.090
after 30 days, your data really
should move off of Standard

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:35.230
and move into Nearline, then
after 90 days to Coldline,

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:37.510
and then after half
a year to Archive--

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:38.900
you can set up those policies.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.680
And then your objects will
automatically tier down

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:42.730
to the appropriate level.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.180
And of course,
because of the changes

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:52.830
that we made with the storage,
with the location types,

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:57.390
you don't have to worry about
losing durability or losing

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:59.700
geo-redundancy as
that tiering happens.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.070
So if you choose a
Multi-Regional location,

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:05.140
for example, your data will
stay geo-redundant the whole way

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:05.640
down.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:09.360
So that means consistent
availability characteristics

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:13.190
and consistent durability.

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.760
So now I want to talk a
little bit about application

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:18.770
of some of these storage
classes to workloads.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:20.480
So I'll take you
through content serving.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.420
And then pass the
baton over to Mike,

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.430
who will take the
presentation over from there.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:28.330
So for content
serving, there actually

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:30.280
some interesting cases.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.370
We have a lot of customers
that do direct serving.

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:36.160
But some use Cloud CDNs.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.650
Some use Custom Front-ends,
but before serving the data.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:42.550
And we also have
compelling use cases

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.850
with our independent
content delivery partners,

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:50.360
where they may be taking over
the last mile distribution.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.110
And these all can mean some
slightly different things

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:58.000
for what storage class you
might ideally want to select.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:02.050
So multi-regions see the
most use for direct serving.

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:06.720
And this is because if you're
serving to either the whole US

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.540
or even to the whole
globe, you don't really

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.340
care about where
the data is located.

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:14.800
You just want geo-redundancy
for the highest availability,

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:20.490
and you want it to be taking
advantage of all of the caching

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.450
that Google makes available.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:25.530
So we have cache control
headers that allow you to state,

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:26.760
to control that caching.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:30.180
But generally speaking, as
soon as an object has already

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.280
been read in a particular
point of presence,

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.740
it will be cached there
and very low latency

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.330
for subsequent accesses.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.780
So we do encourage you to take
advantage of the cache control

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.120
headers for direct serving.

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:45.400
To optimize direct
serving further,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.230
you can take advantage
of our Cloud CDN product.

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:51.310
And what this allows you
to do is basically avoid

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:56.260
a lot of the more
expensive egress pricing

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:59.590
in the cases of cache hits.

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:01.090
And the other part
of what this does

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:04.930
is it allows you to
get HTTPS in custom

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:08.470
domains, some flexibility
on the URL side.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.030
But fundamentally, it just
takes the caching capability

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:14.940
that's already there
in the basic product

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:16.690
and gives you a little
bit more capability

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.760
and some opportunity
for some cost

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.860
savings on the egress side.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:23.440
So that's very
commonly used as part

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:26.650
of the direct serving model.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.650
So so far, that's pretty much
all a multi-regional use case.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:33.910
Custom Front-ends is where
multi-regions may no longer

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:35.630
make as much sense.

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:37.740
So this is where you
want to actually put

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:40.060
VMs in front of your content
serving, because you're

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:42.340
going to manipulate
the data in some way

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:44.710
before it gets sent
out to your customers.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:47.770
And in that case,
the overall latency

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.320
from storage to your
customers access

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:53.260
is both the latency from
storage and then the latency

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:54.430
from the VM.

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:58.300
And so if you want to
minimize the overall latency,

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.250
it's important to
actually co-locate

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:03.410
the storage with the VMs
that are accessing it.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:06.670
So here, you want to either
use a region or a dual region

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.339
to store the data
ideally, so that you'll

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:11.980
know that the storage is local
to the region in which it's

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.900
being accessed by
your front-ends.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:16.190
If you want geo-redundancy,
use a dual region.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.450
And then you can have your
VMs in both of the regions.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:22.030
If it's very low
performance, you may still

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:23.355
get away with multi-regions.

00:17:23.355 --> 00:17:24.730
So it's not a hard
and fast rule.

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:26.690
But generally speaking,
you might be better off

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:27.940
with regions and dual regions.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:34.070
And then you can also use
independent content delivery

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.250
networks.

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:37.970
Some of our partners are
shown here on this slide.

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:41.510
It may be that they have
particular APIs, feature sets

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:44.120
that are particularly
well-aligned to the market

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.520
that the content
is being served to.

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:48.920
It may be that the
particular pops that they

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:52.520
have are in better alignment
or maybe the egress pricing

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:53.960
offers some advantages.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:55.130
It depends on the scenario.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.830
But we work very closely
with these partners,

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:01.550
and it's a great alternative
if, for some reason,

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:06.170
the direct serving or our own
CDN capabilities fall short.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:09.080
So here you would want to use
regional or dual regions, again

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:11.180
for the same reason.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.040
Typically, there
will be something

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:16.880
that's called an origin shield
that's placed between Google

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:20.090
and that CDN partner.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.130
And what the origin shield
does is it basically

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:26.450
ensures that the cache filling
is going to that origin shield

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.220
directly from Google.

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:31.970
And it prevents a ton of access
from going back and hitting

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:33.150
Google at the same time.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.290
So it ends up being the
intermediary between Google

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:37.830
and the CDN partner.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:40.820
So if you have that origin
shield layer co-located

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.560
as closely as possible
with the storage,

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.110
again, you help with
the overall latency.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:49.940
So at this point I'd
like to pass the baton

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.915
to Mike Yu, who will take
you through the next section.

00:18:52.915 --> 00:18:53.540
MIKE YU: Right.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:54.510
Thanks, Geoff.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:55.010
OK.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:57.590
So we're going to quickly go
through storage class selection

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:00.150
and location type selection
for computing and analytics.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.733
And then I'll talk a little bit
about some general performance

00:19:02.733 --> 00:19:04.070
and scaling tips.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:07.310
So one of the things I think is
a little bit interesting here

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.530
is that in your computing
analytics pipelines,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:11.350
there's actually
several types of data.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.737
And your choice of location type
depends on the type of data.

00:19:14.737 --> 00:19:16.320
So first of all, you
have source data.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:18.830
This is data that's
typically persistent

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:21.860
and you need to load up when
you start up your pipeline.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:23.240
And here, what's
really important

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:24.510
is very high throughput.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.320
So you want co-location between
your workers and your data.

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:32.040
So region and dual
region make sense.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.100
The second type is
intermediate data.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.640
And here, this one is a
little bit interesting.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.590
This is data that's produced
by one stage of your pipeline

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:41.240
and consumed by the next.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.730
And it doesn't actually always
make sense to use GCS here.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:46.640
We've actually found a lot
of users who need higher

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:48.920
throughput, lower latency.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:50.780
And this data is
very short-lived.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:53.873
And so sometimes using something
like local SSD makes sense.

00:19:53.873 --> 00:19:55.790
There are also sometimes
specialized services,

00:19:55.790 --> 00:20:01.430
like a shuffle service, that you
can get a higher performance.

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:05.020
The third type of data is what
I call side inputs and staging

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:05.780
data.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:09.350
So this is data that's
typically read once per worker.

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:11.290
It's loaded all up together.

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:13.300
But then after that,
it's never read again.

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:15.730
And here, the type of
location doesn't really

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:16.690
matter that much.

00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:19.510
And the reason why
is that GCS ends up

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:21.290
doing a lot of caching
behind the scenes.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:23.530
And so you're fetching the
same data over and over.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.270
So even if it's
remote the first time,

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:29.700
subsequent times are
typically nice and fast.

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:32.790
And then finally,
there's final outputs.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:35.160
What does your pipeline
actually produce?

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.080
And here, the location
type and source class

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:38.010
really depends on
what you're going

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.458
to do with the final output.

00:20:39.458 --> 00:20:41.750
So if you're producing, say,
process images that you're

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:44.620
going to serve out
to internet users,

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.620
you'd probably want to
use the advice that Geoff

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.200
gave on content serving and
choose a storage class based

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:50.198
off of that.

00:20:50.198 --> 00:20:51.990
If instead, this is
going to be data that's

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.940
going to be fed into
another pipeline,

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:56.093
then it would end up acting
like source data again.

00:20:56.093 --> 00:20:58.260
And you would want to use
regional or dual regional.

00:21:00.998 --> 00:21:02.290
I wanted to give a quick blurb.

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.770
Because we've been
seeing a lot of interest

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.900
in using-- moving Hadoop
workloads to the Cloud.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.750
And so I wanted to
give a shout out

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:13.930
to our Cloud DataProc service,
which is a managed Hadoop

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:14.950
service.

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.260
It lets you bring up
clusters very, very quickly.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:19.090
And we also have
a connector that

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:23.620
allows you to access your source
data and your final outputs

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:27.160
directly from GCS without having
to load it first into HFS.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:31.030
So you can save a bit of
time by skipping that step.

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:32.650
There are a couple of gotchas.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:35.840
Object storage is
not a file system.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:37.850
So we don't have
directory semantics today.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:40.570
So you're going to want to
work around those and eliminate

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.580
your dependency on atomic
directory operations.

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:46.120
So for example, one
common pattern that we see

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:49.555
is that you might
write data temporarily

00:21:49.555 --> 00:21:50.680
into a temporary directory.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:52.430
And then when your
data set is complete,

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:54.928
you'll rename it to
a final directory.

00:21:54.928 --> 00:21:57.220
And an alternate approach is
to go ahead and write that

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:58.630
into the final directory.

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:00.088
And when your data
set is complete,

00:22:00.088 --> 00:22:04.270
write a sentinel file that says,
hey, I'm done with the data.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:08.380
I'll talk a little bit
in a bit on performance.

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.140
But you're going to see
that object storage is

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:14.913
going to have higher latency
overall compared to HFS.

00:22:14.913 --> 00:22:17.080
And so you're going to want
to favor parallelization

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.460
to get around that.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.380
And then finally, like
I already mentioned,

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:25.010
you may want to use local
SSD for temporary data.

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:25.510
OK.

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.970
So let's now talk about
some performance and scaling

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.620
tips, which I think are really
applicable across multiple use

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:31.120
cases.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:36.120
So I think the first
thing to understand

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.250
is the general performance
characteristics

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:39.480
of object storage.

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:45.780
And the TLDR is that
latency for small objects

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:47.223
is relatively high.

00:22:47.223 --> 00:22:49.140
So at 95th percentile,
you might expect, like,

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:52.020
a one byte read to take about
100 milliseconds, give or take.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.420
It depends a bit on the
hotness of the object,

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:57.980
but around 100 milliseconds,
and similarly for writes.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.480
On the flipside, I think where
our strengths are, of course,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.790
horizontal scalability and
then single stream throughput.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:05.890
So with a object
that's large enough,

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.430
you should be able to get
about 800 megabits or about

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.180
100 megabytes per second.

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:14.970
And for writes, you can get
about 400 megabits per second.

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:18.075
So what this means
is that you're

00:23:18.075 --> 00:23:20.450
going to really want to try
to favor larger object sizes.

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:24.170
If you have options in terms
of how to pack your data,

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.850
don't shard them unnecessarily.

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:27.743
Try to keep the objects
relatively large.

00:23:27.743 --> 00:23:29.660
Because you can see on
the graph on the right,

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:32.330
you're not really able to take
advantage of the single stream

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:35.402
throughput until you get
to larger object sizes.

00:23:35.402 --> 00:23:37.610
Similarly, even if you are
able to pack all your data

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:41.040
into larger objects, if
you have structure data,

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.317
you may have internal
segments or pages.

00:23:43.317 --> 00:23:44.900
And if you can, you
want to tune those

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:46.340
to be a little bit larger.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:47.905
We recommend about
two megabytes.

00:23:47.905 --> 00:23:49.530
If you can go larger,
it's even better.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.370
But it's sort of a trade
off between how much you

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:55.760
read at once and how
much effort is wasted,

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:57.560
depending on your query.

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:00.350
And then, like I mentioned
earlier, sometimes you

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.770
can't create larger objects.

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:05.490
You have no choice,
and that's OK.

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:07.670
Just make sure you
parallelize so that you

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:11.035
work around the inherent
latency that's in the service

00:24:11.035 --> 00:24:13.160
and that you can increase
your throughput that way.

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:19.140
So next, let's talk a little
bit about writing objects

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:21.240
and how to get the best
performance out of writes.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.162
There's actually three
high level strategies

00:24:23.162 --> 00:24:24.120
you can use for writes.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.033
The simplest one is just
a non-resumable upload.

00:24:27.033 --> 00:24:28.200
You use your single request.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:30.610
You upload your entire object.

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:33.810
The second strategy is to do
what we call resumable upload.

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:36.570
So we have a protocol where
you first send a request

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.240
to initiate a new
resumable upload session,

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:41.280
and you get back a
unique session ID.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:43.230
And then you issue
a second request

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.600
where you upload your data.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.220
And hopefully that succeeds.

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:48.720
But if it fails,
what you can end up

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.350
doing is you can query
the server and say,

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.683
hey, what was the last offset
that you were successfully

00:24:52.683 --> 00:24:53.363
able to commit?

00:24:53.363 --> 00:24:54.780
And once you have
that offset, you

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:58.620
can then issue another
upload request and only

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:02.960
upload the bytes that the
server hasn't received.

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:05.930
The third approach
is parallel upload.

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:09.800
So you can split your
object into parts,

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:12.050
you can upload them, and
then you can do a compose.

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:14.420
The compose operation is
a metadata only operation,

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:16.970
so it's constant time.

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:19.310
It doesn't rewrite
any of the bytes.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.810
And if you have your
entire object up front,

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:25.420
you can, of course, split it
upfront and upload those parts.

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:28.070
You can get the sharding
done right away.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:29.810
If you're doing a
streaming approach

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:32.090
and you don't know what
the final object size is,

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:35.780
you can iteratively
chunk your buffer, upload

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.000
that in chunks,
compose, and then

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.830
do it again for the next
set of bytes in your buffer.

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:44.800
So obviously, you want to
use non-resumable uploads

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:48.190
by default. But I think
what's not really always

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:51.360
clear is how high of a bar you
want to use before you switch

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:53.740
over to resumable upload.

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:58.043
Resumable uploads have
several disadvantages.

00:25:58.043 --> 00:25:59.710
One of the big ones
is that they require

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:01.253
a minimum of two requests.

00:26:01.253 --> 00:26:03.670
And if you imagine that that
first request takes something

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:05.920
on the order of, say,
100 milliseconds, that

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:08.170
means for small objects,
that 100 milliseconds

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:11.590
is a large part of the
overall upload time.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:13.650
So it's a large
amount of overhead.

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.400
So the general rule
of thumb I like to use

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.410
is take your expected
throughput, multiply it

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.290
by about 30 seconds, and
that's a reasonable cutoff

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:24.490
time for the object size
before you want to switch over

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:25.920
to resumable uploads.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:27.420
What that means is
that you're going

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.970
to lose, at most, 30 seconds of
effort if something goes wrong.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:34.420
And things go wrong
very rarely usually.

00:26:34.420 --> 00:26:37.390
It also means that, as you
hit that 30 second threshold,

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:41.410
the amount of overhead that
that first request adds

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:43.692
is relatively small,
less than half a percent.

00:26:43.692 --> 00:26:45.400
And so to give you
some concrete numbers,

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:47.020
if you have clients
that are using,

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:50.710
say, cable, cable
modem access, and they

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:52.990
have a four megabit per
second upload speed,

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:55.360
then the cutoff is going
to be about 15 megabytes.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:58.490
Similarly, if you have a
VM that's on our network,

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.593
you're getting the full
400 megabits per second,

00:27:00.593 --> 00:27:03.010
you shouldn't really be even
considering resumable uploads

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:04.960
till about over a gig or so.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:07.390
So it's fairly large
objects before you really

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:10.178
want to care about this.

00:27:10.178 --> 00:27:12.470
And then finally, if resumable
uploads have a high bar,

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:14.780
I would say parallel uploads
have an even higher bar.

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:17.790
You really only want-- there's
a lot of additional complexity

00:27:17.790 --> 00:27:19.190
that you add with this.

00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:21.850
So it's not something
you want do lightly.

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:24.600
You should really only do this
if you are uploading from a VM

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.200
and you really need greater
than 400 megabits per second.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.870
That typically means you're
talking very, very large object

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:33.600
sizes, right, tens
of gigabytes or more.

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.030
There is one other
use case, which

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:38.750
is that certain connections
have relatively high bandwidth,

00:27:38.750 --> 00:27:40.670
but a relatively high delay.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:45.560
And due to the way GCP
works, you sometimes

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:46.930
are not able to fill your pipe.

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:49.250
In those cases, sometimes
having multiple connections

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:50.352
is beneficial.

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:55.410
So next, I want to
talk about retries.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:59.020
It sounds a little bit dry, but
it actually has a big impact

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.240
on both the reliability
and the performance

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:02.630
that you're going to get.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.880
So right here, I have a
pretty typical retry strategy.

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:07.220
It's exponential back off.

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:08.220
We have a loop.

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.070
We make a call.

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:10.277
There's a deadline.

00:28:10.277 --> 00:28:12.610
If there's a retriable error,
we back off exponentially,

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:16.210
and we give up after a
fixed number of attempts.

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:19.490
So there's, I think, two
tricky things to consider here.

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:22.210
So first of all, right
now with every attempt,

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:24.208
we're reissuing
the same request.

00:28:24.208 --> 00:28:25.750
And so there's a
question of, is that

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:29.240
the right strategy for
getting the best reliability?

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.030
The second question is,
we have this deadline.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.280
So what's the right deadline?

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:35.900
If you do it too
short or too long,

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.670
that has impact on
performance or reliability.

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.940
So let's look into
those one by one.

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:45.230
So first of all, I think
the key thing with retries

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:48.540
is you really want to be
failing over if at all possible.

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:50.840
So this is sort of
a simplified view

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:54.713
of what Cloud storage might look
like in this implementation.

00:28:54.713 --> 00:28:55.880
We have a set of front-ends.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:58.297
They're running on multiple
servers and they're stateless,

00:28:58.297 --> 00:29:00.417
so you can really hit
any of those servers.

00:29:00.417 --> 00:29:02.000
Now we have a set
of metadata servers,

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:03.530
and those are stateful.

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:05.270
And so for any given
object, you need

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:07.970
to go to a specific
subset of those servers.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:09.780
And similarly, we
have data servers.

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:11.630
And you can only use
a specific subset.

00:29:11.630 --> 00:29:14.470
So those are
represented in blue.

00:29:14.470 --> 00:29:17.710
So imagine a user makes a
request for a specific object.

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:18.960
It hits one of the front-ends.

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:20.390
It hits one of the
metadata servers.

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:21.723
It hits one of the data servers.

00:29:21.723 --> 00:29:23.240
And something goes wrong.

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:26.562
Well, if the error happens at
the metadata or data level,

00:29:26.562 --> 00:29:28.020
then Cloud storage
is going to take

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:29.690
care of retrying automatically.

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:32.330
And it's going to failover
to the alternate replica.

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:34.530
So this should all
happen under the hoods.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:37.470
So that's great, because
it means we're not retrying

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:40.200
the same unhealthy node, right?

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:43.050
The problem is what happens
if the front-end itself

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:44.100
is unhealthy.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:46.380
A naive retry
strategy would end up

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:49.590
reusing the same TCP connection
or the same HTTP connection,

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.650
and you'll end up hitting
the same unhealthy front-end.

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:55.570
So it's pretty important
that when you do your retry,

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:58.650
make sure you're actually
opening up a new connection.

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:00.960
Our load balancing will
typically send that

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:02.940
to a different server.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:05.820
And you're unlikely-- and that
will cause proper failover.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:11.490
So let's also talk
about deadlines now.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.520
I mentioned that if it's
too short or too long,

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:14.740
there's repercussions.

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:17.370
So if you're setting that
deadline that's too short,

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:21.660
the biggest problem is that
you might be prematurely

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.220
canceling or failing
a request that

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:25.560
could have succeeded if you
were just willing to wait

00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:26.760
a little bit longer.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.790
And that ends up driving up
your error rate artificially.

00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:32.488
It ends up wasting work, right?

00:30:32.488 --> 00:30:34.530
Similarly, if you set a
deadline that's too long,

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:36.990
the problem is that you
may sit around waiting

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:40.180
too long for a request that's
never going to succeed.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:42.570
And if you had just
retried a little earlier,

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:46.020
you could have really
cut down that latency.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:47.850
So what do you do?

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:50.170
What we recommend
doing is a technique

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:51.810
called hedged requests.

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:54.270
The problem with the
previous algorithm

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:57.570
is that each attempt
had to run individually.

00:30:57.570 --> 00:30:59.110
They couldn't run in parallel.

00:30:59.110 --> 00:31:02.340
So if you instead issue your
retries in parallel, what that

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:04.560
allows you to do is
each individual attempt

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.980
can have a long
deadline so that we

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:12.240
wait for a long enough time in
case the whole service is slow.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.390
And at the same time,
you can shorten the delay

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:18.750
before issuing that second
request so that you can really

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:20.880
cut down on your tail agency.

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:23.340
So I can walk through this
in a little bit more detail

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:24.810
to hopefully make it more clear.

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:28.650
So imagine you issue
your initial attempt.

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:31.260
And it's taking a little
while to come back.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:34.800
In fact, it's a
little beyond normal.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:37.050
One good way to
implement this is

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:39.630
to track what your
typical P95 latency

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:42.300
is, say over the
last minute or two.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:44.670
And so you wait, and you
haven't yet received a response

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:47.880
after hitting your P95 latency.

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:50.700
So what you can go ahead
and do is issue that retry,

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:51.580
that second attempt.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:52.890
But don't cancel the first one.

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:54.840
Let it keep going, right?

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:58.200
And if this is a scenario where
that first attempt happened

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:01.080
to hit a slow node and
it's just taken a while

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:03.180
and the second attempt
fails over properly,

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:06.150
then you'd expect
that second attempt

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:08.508
to succeed pretty quickly.

00:32:08.508 --> 00:32:10.800
And so you've really cut down
your latency quite a bit.

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.685
If, on the other hand, let's
suppose this first request

00:32:16.685 --> 00:32:18.310
was taking a long
time, not because you

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:21.310
hit a by a single slow
node, but maybe the service

00:32:21.310 --> 00:32:22.990
is having issues overall.

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:24.730
Well, because you've
set a long deadline

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:27.430
for each of these requests, you
didn't cancel that first one.

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:29.930
And so that allows that
first request to succeed.

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:31.450
It will be, of course, slow.

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:34.450
But at least you're not
wasting work or throwing away,

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:38.470
causing errors prematurely.

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:39.470
So that's head requests.

00:32:39.470 --> 00:32:41.220
And I wanted to throw
out two other things

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.110
that I think are pretty
important, especially

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:44.700
if you do use head requests.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:47.160
I think the first one
is to make sure to limit

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.170
your retry volume.

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:52.950
And of course, this helps
us, the service, from getting

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:54.670
slammed when things
are unhealthy.

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:56.290
But it also helps your client.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:33:00.030
And if you noticed in the
previous hedged request slides,

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.030
I suggested you waiting
for the P95 latency

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.710
before issuing
the head requests.

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:06.335
That means in steady
state, you're only

00:33:06.335 --> 00:33:09.120
issuing about 5% extra traffic.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.330
But the problem is that
latency can temporarily spike.

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:13.830
If it temporarily
spikes, then you

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:16.830
could be issuing up
to 100% extra traffic.

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:18.360
And so limiting
your retry volume

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:23.830
to a smaller subset, say 10%,
is generally a good practice.

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:26.160
The other thing to be
careful about with retries

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:28.240
is making sure you have
idempotent sequences.

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:32.460
This is something that we've
seen a few different users

00:33:32.460 --> 00:33:33.150
forget about.

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:35.740
And it can cause serious
problems, like data loss.

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:38.520
So I thought it was worth
going into that really quickly

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:39.700
to explore that.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:43.020
So a non-idempotent sequence is
a sequence of operations where,

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:46.470
if you repeat the entire
sequence or a subsequence--

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:51.693
it's idempotent if you always
end up with the same state.

00:33:51.693 --> 00:33:53.610
It's non-idempotent if
it's possible to end up

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:54.940
in a different state.

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:57.420
So here's an example of a
non-idempotent sequence.

00:33:57.420 --> 00:33:59.820
Imagine we create
an Object X and that

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:01.500
creates Generation Number 1.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:04.507
Then we issue a delete
operation, which deletes it.

00:34:04.507 --> 00:34:06.090
And then we follow
that up by creating

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:07.730
a new version of that object.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.330
So that's Generation Number 2.

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:12.733
So this shows what happens
in that sort of normal case

00:34:12.733 --> 00:34:13.650
where no errors occur.

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:20.830
So here's an example of what
can happen if an error occurs

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:22.270
and you issue a retry.

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:25.540
So imagine that the
initial create succeeds.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:28.370
Your initial delete
request takes a long time,

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:30.340
and so you time out.

00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:32.920
And when you time out,
you issue that retry,

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:34.670
and that retry succeeds.

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:37.870
So now you've deleted
Generation Number 1.

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:39.820
You then go ahead and
create the object again.

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:42.880
And you've created
Generation Number 2.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:46.000
And the problem is, is that
very first delete attempt,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.528
even though you timed it
out on the client side,

00:34:48.528 --> 00:34:50.320
it was actually stuck
in one of the buffers

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:52.480
in our server, one of
the queues in our server.

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:54.639
And so it does end up
getting applied and ends up

00:34:54.639 --> 00:34:58.160
deleting the Generation 2
object that you just created.

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:00.950
So this is a data loss scenario.

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:03.470
So you really want to make
sure that your sequences are

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:05.890
idempotent to avoid
this type of problem.

00:35:05.890 --> 00:35:08.420
One way of doing that
is to use preconditions.

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:11.552
So in this example, we
can really make sure.

00:35:11.552 --> 00:35:13.010
Rather than saying
delete Object X,

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:15.980
we can say delete Object
X Generation 1 only.

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:19.640
And that ensures that that dark
request that came in afterwards

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:22.220
or that finished
afterwards gets canceled.

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:24.720
Because it is no longer relevant
to the current generation

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:27.920
of the object, which
is Generation 2.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:30.315
There are other
ways of doing this.

00:35:30.315 --> 00:35:31.940
Another common
technique for doing this

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.300
is to simply never overwrite
objects with new content.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:35.823
Right?

00:35:35.823 --> 00:35:36.740
So you can write once.

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:39.170
And if you ever have
to rewrite, it's

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:42.760
always rewriting
the same content.

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:44.260
OK.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:46.180
Finally, I wanted
to end and talk

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:50.350
a little bit about
scaling and hotspotting.

00:35:50.350 --> 00:35:53.350
And generally speaking,
scaling is something

00:35:53.350 --> 00:35:54.910
that Cloud Storage
does pretty well.

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.100
You don't typically have
to worry about your data

00:35:57.100 --> 00:36:00.130
size or the object count.

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:03.680
We just scale horizontally,
usually without worry.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:06.220
But there is one case that
we have seen a lot of folks

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:07.610
get tripped up on.

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:10.000
And that is localized
hotspotting,

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.310
which can also result in
scaling bottlenecks, as well.

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:16.360
And so I wanted to give a little
bit of intuition and background

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:19.600
for what's happening under the
hoods and then, some advice

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:22.780
on it on how to
work around this.

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:28.870
So Cloud Storage uses a
scaling or sharding strategy

00:36:28.870 --> 00:36:30.053
called range-based sharding.

00:36:30.053 --> 00:36:31.720
And so what that means
is we, of course,

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:34.870
store your objects
on multiple servers.

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:37.000
And the way we assign
objects to servers

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:40.840
is we split up your object
namespace into ranges.

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:42.820
So in this example,
an object starting

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:46.210
with C would go into that
first shard and an object

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:50.450
starting with M would go
into the second shard.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:53.870
And we chose this strategy for a
very specific purpose, which is

00:36:53.870 --> 00:36:56.930
to provide consistent listing.

00:36:56.930 --> 00:37:00.680
So imagine a user issues a
request to list every object

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:02.840
starting with t/.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:06.030
In order to fill this
request, it's pretty simple.

00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:09.950
The request needs to get
routed to a single shard,

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:16.070
this S shard, and that shard
is sufficient to service

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:16.880
the entire request.

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:19.400
We know that there are no
objects starting with t

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:21.990
in any of the other shards.

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:25.820
So let's look at an
alternative approach

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:27.260
to what we could have done.

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:29.540
The alternate approach
is to do something

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:32.120
like hash-based sharding,
where you take the object name,

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:34.678
you hash it, and
then you assign it

00:37:34.678 --> 00:37:36.470
to one of the shards
based off of the hash.

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:39.810
And that really distributes
the objects all over the place.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:44.340
So this same listing
operation now, what happens?

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:46.050
In order to service
a request, you

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:48.610
have to look at every
single shard, right?

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:53.010
Because you don't know where
the t/ objects are going to be.

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:55.720
And what happens
if a shard is slow?

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:57.300
You have to wait for it.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:59.107
So as the number of
shards increases,

00:37:59.107 --> 00:38:00.690
you're going to hit
that tail latency.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:04.920
Your overall listing
latency is going to go up.

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:09.325
Perhaps even worse, what happens
if one of the shards fails?

00:38:09.325 --> 00:38:11.200
Then you're stuck with
a sort of poor choice.

00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:13.140
You can either return
incomplete results

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:15.090
or you can fail the
overall request.

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:16.740
And again, as the
number of shards

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:20.370
increases, assuming you're
failing the overall request,

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:23.060
your chance of
success goes way down.

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:26.000
So because of this,
hash-based sharding really

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:28.370
doesn't scale well when
you're trying to provide

00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:29.930
strongly consistent listing.

00:38:29.930 --> 00:38:31.763
And so we've gone with
range-based sharding.

00:38:33.950 --> 00:38:37.540
So of course, one of
your shards can get hot.

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:40.060
And we do auto scale,
and we detect that,

00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:43.980
and we split those
shards in half or more

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:45.760
to try to distribute load.

00:38:45.760 --> 00:38:50.290
And I wanted to give a feel
for when this becomes an issue.

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:54.010
You should expect about 1,000
object writes per second

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:56.160
and about 5,000
reads per second.

00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:57.910
That's probably a
little bit conservative.

00:38:57.910 --> 00:39:01.810
But that's roughly what you
can expect for any given shard.

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:04.120
And then, when the
shard gets hot,

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:06.760
we try to split ahead of time
so that you don't notice things.

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:09.350
But you can expect a
delay of about 20 minutes

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:11.080
if you are slamming
a particular shard.

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:13.090
That's also, again, probably
a little bit conservative.

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:14.800
But I think it's
good to keep in mind,

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:16.717
because this is about
when you're going to run

00:39:16.717 --> 00:39:18.922
into these type of problems.

00:39:18.922 --> 00:39:20.380
And in order for
this to work well,

00:39:20.380 --> 00:39:22.213
I think the key thing
is to make sure you're

00:39:22.213 --> 00:39:23.510
distributing your load evenly.

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:25.780
If you have a nice,
consistent, even load,

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:28.150
then we can really detect
shards getting hot.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:31.120
And we can split them well ahead
of that becoming a problem.

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:39.550
So there is one common pattern
that essentially trips up

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:42.410
a lot of uses.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:44.590
And that's sequential access.

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:47.180
And let's go through
why this is problematic.

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:50.170
So by sequential access,
I mean reading or writing

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:54.590
objects in order of their name,
right, in lexicographic order.

00:39:54.590 --> 00:39:59.900
And in this example, we have g,
h, i, j, k, so pretty simple.

00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:02.540
And you can see that these
accesses are distributed

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:05.240
across two range-based shards.

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:07.630
But the biggest problem
here is that, even

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:10.130
though overall the accesses are
distributed across those two

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:13.940
shards, if you take a look
at any particular time range,

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:16.620
it's always lasering
one shard at a time.

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:18.650
So you don't actually get
the scaling properties

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:20.070
of having two different shards.

00:40:20.070 --> 00:40:24.740
You're really bound by the
limit of a single shard.

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:26.550
So that's what we
mean by hotspotting.

00:40:26.550 --> 00:40:28.133
You're really lasering
this one shard.

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:33.600
A potential way around this is
to go ahead and say, OK, well,

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:36.450
let's split these shards up
into smaller ranges, right?

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:40.020
And this definitely does
help spread the load.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:42.240
But it causes fragmentation.

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:44.070
And fragmentation
is a problem, again,

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:46.680
because of consistent listing.

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:49.470
Previously, with a
low fragmentation,

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:52.290
the list operation can hit
a relatively few number

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:53.960
of shards.

00:40:53.960 --> 00:40:57.470
With high fragmentation, you
end up having to hit many shards

00:40:57.470 --> 00:40:59.270
and you start running
into the same tail

00:40:59.270 --> 00:41:01.460
latency and
availability problems

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:03.510
that you would get with
hash-based sharding.

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:05.595
So I wanted to go
through this because this

00:41:05.595 --> 00:41:07.970
is sort of a fundamental trade
off that you have to make,

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:10.100
and it's pretty difficult
to get around this.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:15.320
So here are some
common naming patterns

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:19.890
that we see that cause this
type of sequential access.

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:23.060
So one of them is
using year, month, day.

00:41:23.060 --> 00:41:26.810
You see this a lot with
writing logs, for example.

00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:29.750
You also see things like
having order numbers or user

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:33.680
IDs that are sequential, just
counting up monotonically.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:37.090
And then, similarly, we
also see use of timestamps.

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:38.810
So these are three
common patterns

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:41.625
that we see that
can cause problems.

00:41:41.625 --> 00:41:43.250
There are a couple
of ways you can work

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:44.400
around this if you need to.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:46.127
And again, I want
to re-emphasize,

00:41:46.127 --> 00:41:47.960
it really only becomes
a problem after 1,000

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:50.150
writes per second or
5,000 reads per second.

00:41:50.150 --> 00:41:53.670
So oftentimes this
isn't an issue.

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:56.025
One of the things
you can do is--

00:41:56.025 --> 00:41:58.040
I'm just going to call
it, like, manually

00:41:58.040 --> 00:41:59.850
partitioning your prefixes.

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:03.080
So in this example, we
pulled the log type upfront.

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:05.390
And that means that if I'm
processing a single day

00:42:05.390 --> 00:42:06.890
across multiple log
types, I'm going

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:09.120
to end up hitting
multiple prefixes at once,

00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:11.330
and then I'm going
to get a fixed

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:13.330
multiplication of the sharding.

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:22.400
The second approach is to
just don't use sequential IDs

00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:23.422
if you don't have to.

00:42:23.422 --> 00:42:25.130
So for order numbers,
if you don't really

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:27.300
need that count of
the number of orders,

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:29.360
for example, just use
a uuid and that'll

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:32.530
spread the load more evenly.

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:34.270
The third approach
is you can end up

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:36.280
taking a hash of
your object name

00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:38.403
and putting that
into your prefix.

00:42:38.403 --> 00:42:39.820
And so in this
particular example,

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:41.070
I didn't hash the entire name.

00:42:41.070 --> 00:42:44.380
But I hashed timestamp,
and I put that up front.

00:42:44.380 --> 00:42:49.060
And this gives you really
good, even load balancing.

00:42:49.060 --> 00:42:53.410
The big drawback is that you
no longer get in order listing.

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:55.960
So again, this only
works if you don't really

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:57.930
need to list in order.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:00.260
And finally, there
is an alternative

00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:02.930
that you can do that's
a little bit in between.

00:43:02.930 --> 00:43:06.933
You can hash, and then map it
to a fixed number of shards.

00:43:06.933 --> 00:43:08.600
And what this ends
up allowing you to do

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.300
is it allows you to preserve
the in order listing

00:43:11.300 --> 00:43:16.190
by issuing 10 listing operations
in parallel, one to each shard,

00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:17.190
and merging the results.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:17.982
So it's extra work.

00:43:17.982 --> 00:43:20.480
But if in order listing is
absolutely something you need,

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:23.414
and you also need to get
around the scaling limitations,

00:43:23.414 --> 00:43:24.997
this is an approach
that you can take.

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:29.780
Finally, I wanted to mention--

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:35.150
all the previous alternatives
I mentioned were around naming.

00:43:35.150 --> 00:43:37.190
You don't always have to
actually change a name.

00:43:37.190 --> 00:43:39.350
In the case of bulk
operations, you

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:42.680
can simply make sure that
your access is spread evenly

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:43.250
and randomly.

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:46.220
So for example, if I'm
running a batch job

00:43:46.220 --> 00:43:50.240
across my entire corpus, I
could have the different workers

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:53.900
process different
directories simultaneously

00:43:53.900 --> 00:43:56.250
and that would cause
more even load.

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:58.615
You can also randomly choose
which objects to process,

00:43:58.615 --> 00:44:00.740
and that will distribute
the load a little bit more

00:44:00.740 --> 00:44:03.120
evenly, as well.

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:08.210
So with that, hopefully
you found it useful.

00:44:08.210 --> 00:44:11.600
That's the main tips that
we wanted to provide today.

00:44:11.600 --> 00:44:13.820
We didn't cover a
lot of other areas.

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.000
I know there's a lot
of tips in security.

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:17.755
There's a lot of
tips around archival.

00:44:17.755 --> 00:44:19.130
So I wanted to
give a quick plug.

00:44:19.130 --> 00:44:22.350
We have 10 other storage
talks that are ongoing.

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:25.070
The ones in blue, these are
ones that haven't been held yet.

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:26.960
So if you want to catch
them live and they're

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:29.455
interesting to you, I'd
encourage you to do that.

00:44:29.455 --> 00:44:30.830
The ones that
aren't highlighted,

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:31.872
they've already happened.

00:44:31.872 --> 00:44:34.010
But they should be on
YouTube pretty soon.

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:38.640
And so there's good
tips on, for example,

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:42.140
seeing how Twitter is
migrating their workload to us

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:44.750
for a very high intensity
computer use case

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:49.130
and also how to do things like
transferring your data in.

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:50.720
Hopefully everyone
found that useful.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:52.890
Geoff and I are going
to hang out afterwards.

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:55.440
And we're happy to answer
questions that you might have.

00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:56.420
So thank you.

